An exact symbolic interpreter over a deterministic scalar language
promises more than a candidate input that reaches the last line.
The valuation should reproduce the entire recorded branch prefix
and give the same value at the selected return. Use a separate
concrete interpreter for replay, with exact arithmetic, the same
input precondition, and no hidden inputs or external effects.
Classify a model that fails replay as an interpreter or encoding
disagreement, not as a confirmed contract failure. The contract
itself computes a concrete expected value independently.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Model $m$, symbolic path $p$, return term $t$
\Let{x}{\operatorname{inputFrom}(m)}
\If{not $\operatorname{admissible}(x)$}
  \State \Return invalid input model
\EndIf
\Let{(q,y)}{\operatorname{concreteRun}(x)}
\To{Compare ordered branch identities and outcomes.}
\If{$q\ne p$ or $y\ne\operatorname{evaluate}(t,m)$}
  \State \Return model disagreement
\EndIf
\To{The concrete contract is separate from both interpreters.}
\If{$y\ne\operatorname{expected}(x)$}
  \State \Return confirmed failure at $x$
\EndIf
\State \Return replayed without failure
\end{sudocode}

In the clamping example, input $11$ should record false for both
tests and yield the term value $11$. The concrete expected value is
$10$, so replay confirms a mismatch. Now suppose a query for the
same symbolic return path produces input $12$ because its encoding
forgot the second false branch. A real run records false and then
true, returning $10$. The harness rejects the model for the path
before comparing it to the contract. This distinction makes $12$
a regression for the path encoding, not a reported error in the
clamp at that input.

After replaying a prefix, agreement means the two runs are still
at the same program point for this valuation. Compare branch
identities as well as Boolean outcomes: in a loop two equal
outcomes need not refer to the same dynamic step. The cost of a
replay is one concrete run plus evaluating the symbolic return
term and comparing the trace. Passing such tests checks the
soundness of these individual models; it does not prove that the
engine explored every feasible prefix or that an unsatisfiable
answer was correct.

\section{Concolic Replay and Honest Coverage}

A concolic run maintains both concrete values and symbolic terms, using the concrete values to take branches. Starting at $x=0$ records $X\ge0$ and $X\le11$ for the original candidate. Keeping the prefix and flipping the last outcome produces $X\ge0\land X>11$, with $12$ as one possible next test. To flip the first outcome, drop the later guard altogether: it was evaluated only on paths that passed the first branch. Branch flips supplement, rather than replace, the contract-directed query that found $11$.

Report three kinds of outcomes separately: replay-confirmed violations, paths or contract obligations proved infeasible, and work left unresolved by unsupported operations or budgets. For an external routine executed concretely, recording a single return value is not a symbolic model of that routine for all inputs. Either provide a justified model, restrict the claim to the replayed inputs, or keep the affected path pending. Test an interpreter on small finite functions by enumerating their input domain, checking that each model replays with its recorded branch outcomes, and comparing the discovered contract violations with direct evaluation. An exhausted budget may limit detection even when every reported counterexample is correct.
